The current allowlist still permits `base_ref` values that start with `-` (e.g. `--help`). Since this value is passed as a positional argument to `git diff`, a leading dash can be interpreted as a git option (argument/option injection), changing command behavior. Consider rejecting refs that begin with `-` and/or using a safer verification approach (e.g., verifying the revision with git before using it).
```suggestion
        // Validate base_ref to prevent command/option injection into git
        if (
          params.base_ref.startsWith('-') ||
          !/^[a-zA-Z0-9._\-~^/]+$/.test(params.base_ref)
        ) {
          return {
            error:
              'Invalid base_ref format — must not start with "-" and may contain only alphanumeric, dots, dashes, tildes, carets, and slashes',
          };
```

This regex is stricter than what git accepts as a revision/ref (e.g., `HEAD@{1}` and `main^{}` are valid rev syntaxes but are rejected due to `@`, `{`, `}`). If this tool is meant to accept general "branch/commit" inputs (per the MCP prompt), this validation could break legitimate workflows. Consider switching from a regex allowlist to `git rev-parse --verify` (or broadening the allowlist to cover common rev syntaxes) while still blocking whitespace/control characters.
```suggestion
        // Basic safety: disallow whitespace/control characters to avoid malformed revs
        if (/[ \t\r\n\v\f\x00-\x1F\x7F]/.test(params.base_ref)) {
          return { error: 'Invalid base_ref format — whitespace/control characters are not allowed' };
        }
        // Let git validate the revision syntax (supports full git rev syntax)
        try {
          execFileSync('git', ['rev-parse', '--verify', params.base_ref], {
            cwd: repo.repoPath,
            encoding: 'utf-8',
          });
        } catch {
          return { error: `Invalid base_ref: ${params.base_ref}` };
        }
```

Like the HTTP endpoint, this guard tests the raw query string. That will reject legitimate read-only queries that mention these keywords inside quoted strings (e.g., filtering for a node whose name contains "create"/"delete"). If that’s not intended, consider stripping string literals/comments before matching or tokenizing the query so only real clause/procedure keywords are blocked.
```suggestion
  /**
   * Strip string literals and comments from a Cypher query so that write-pattern
   * detection does not trigger on keywords that only appear in non-executable text.
   */
  private static stripLiteralsAndComments(query: string): string {
    // Remove single-line comments that start with -- and go to the end of the line
    let sanitized = query.replace(/--.*$/gm, '');

    // Remove block comments: /* ... */
    sanitized = sanitized.replace(/\/\*[\s\S]*?\*\//g, '');

    // Replace single-quoted string literals with empty quotes
    sanitized = sanitized.replace(/'([^'\\]|\\.)*'/g, "''");

    // Replace double-quoted string literals with empty quotes
    sanitized = sanitized.replace(/"([^"\\]|\\.)*"/g, '""');

    // Replace backtick-quoted identifiers/strings with empty backticks
    sanitized = sanitized.replace(/`([^`\\]|\\.)*`/g, '``');

    return sanitized;
  }

