The test retrieves the `LocalBackend` instance via `(handle as any)._backend`, where `_backend` is a non-standard property stashed on the handle by `afterSetup`. This pattern bypasses TypeScript's type system entirely, making the code fragile — if `afterSetup` fails silently or the property name changes, `backend` will be `undefined` and tests will fail with a confusing `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined`. Consider extending `IndexedDBHandle` with an optional typed extension field (e.g., via a typed wrapper) or passing the backend through a closure captured in the `fn` callback instead of attaching it to the handle.
```suggestion
      // backend is created in afterSetup and attached to the handle;
      // retrieve it via a typed wrapper and fail fast if missing.
      const handleWithBackend = handle as typeof handle & { _backend?: LocalBackend };
      if (!handleWithBackend._backend) {
        throw new Error('LocalBackend instance was not initialized on test handle (_backend missing)');
      }
      backend = handleWithBackend._backend;
```

The CUDA library probe only checks a few hard-coded paths. On WSL2 with GPU enabled, `libcuda.so.1` is typically under `/usr/lib/wsl/lib/`, and other distros may use `/usr/lib64/` or rely on `ldconfig` cache. With the current list, CUDA-capable systems may be incorrectly detected as unavailable and always fall back to CPU. Consider expanding the paths and/or using `ldconfig -p` (when available) to detect `libcuda.so.1` more reliably.
```suggestion
    // Quick check that the NVIDIA driver stack is present
    execSync('nvidia-smi', { stdio: 'ignore', timeout: 3000 });

    // Common library locations across various distros and WSL2
    const commonPaths = [
      // Debian/Ubuntu default
      '/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libcuda.so',
      '/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libcuda.so.1',
      // WSL2 with GPU enabled
      '/usr/lib/wsl/lib/libcuda.so.1',
      // Common lib64 locations
      '/usr/lib64/libcuda.so',
      '/usr/lib64/libcuda.so.1',
      // Typical CUDA installation dir
      '/usr/local/cuda/lib64/libcuda.so.1',
      '/usr/local/cuda/lib64/libcudart.so',
    ];

    if (commonPaths.some(p => existsSync(p))) {
      return true;
    }

    // Fallback: query dynamic linker cache when available
    try {
      const ldconfigOutput = execSync('ldconfig -p', {
        encoding: 'utf8',
        stdio: ['ignore', 'pipe', 'ignore'],
        timeout: 3000,
      }) as unknown as string;

      // Presence of libcuda.so.1 in the cache is a strong signal CUDA is usable
      return ldconfigOutput.includes('libcuda.so.1');
    } catch {
      // ldconfig may not be present (e.g. some containers); fall through
      return false;
    }
```

The CUDA library probe uses a small hard-coded path list. This will miss common CUDA driver locations like WSL2 (`/usr/lib/wsl/lib/libcuda.so.1`) and some distros (`/usr/lib64/libcuda.so.1`), causing false negatives and forcing CPU even when CUDA is usable. Consider expanding the paths and/or consulting `ldconfig -p` to detect `libcuda.so.1` more reliably.
```suggestion
    // Quick check that an NVIDIA driver is present and responsive
    execSync('nvidia-smi', { stdio: 'ignore', timeout: 3000 });

    // Common locations for CUDA driver/runtime libraries across distros and WSL2
    const commonPaths = [
      // Debian/Ubuntu (driver)
      '/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libcuda.so',
      '/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libcuda.so.1',
      // WSL2
      '/usr/lib/wsl/lib/libcuda.so.1',
      // Generic lib directories
      '/usr/lib/libcuda.so.1',
      '/usr/lib64/libcuda.so.1',
      // Typical CUDA toolkit runtime path
      '/usr/local/cuda/lib64/libcudart.so',
    ];

    if (commonPaths.some((p) => existsSync(p))) {
      return true;
    }

    // Fallback: consult dynamic linker cache for libcuda.so.1
    try {
      execSync('ldconfig -p | grep -q libcuda.so.1', { stdio: 'ignore' });
      return true;
    } catch {
      // ldconfig not available or libcuda.so.1 not found in cache
      return false;
    }
```

**major:** This bare `catch` swallows **all** errors, not just "file not found". If `.gitnexusignore` exists but has permission issues, corrupt encoding, or a disk error, the failure is silently ignored — the user won't know their patterns weren't loaded.

Consider catching only `ENOENT` and re-throwing everything else:

```typescript
} catch (err: unknown) {
  if ((err as NodeJS.ErrnoException).code === 'ENOENT') {
    return null; // No .gitnexusignore file — that's fine
  }
  throw err; // Permission error, corrupt file, etc.
}
```

