The cycle diagnostic is using `lastLevel.length > 1` as a proxy for “cycle files”, but the last topo level can legitimately contain multiple acyclic files (e.g. a graph with only independent roots). This will produce misleading logs in dev. Consider logging cycles only when the sorter actually appended a cycle group (e.g., have `topologicalLevelSort` return `{ levels, cycleFiles }`, or recompute `cycleFiles` here and log based on `cycleFiles.length > 0`).
```suggestion
  // Helper to compute files that participate in import cycles using a Kahn-style algorithm.
  const getImportCycleFiles = (importMap: any): string[] => {
    const inDegree = new Map<string, number>();
    const adjacency = new Map<string, Set<string>>();

    // Build adjacency list and in-degree counts.
    for (const [from, deps] of importMap as Iterable<[string, Iterable<string>]>) {
      if (!adjacency.has(from)) adjacency.set(from, new Set());
      if (!inDegree.has(from)) inDegree.set(from, 0);

      for (const to of deps) {
        let neighbors = adjacency.get(from);
        if (!neighbors) {
          neighbors = new Set<string>();
          adjacency.set(from, neighbors);
        }
        neighbors.add(to);

        if (!inDegree.has(to)) inDegree.set(to, 0);
        inDegree.set(to, (inDegree.get(to) || 0) + 1);
      }
    }

    // Initialize queue with all nodes that have no incoming edges.
    const queue: string[] = [];
    for (const [node, deg] of inDegree) {
      if (deg === 0) queue.push(node);
    }

    // Remove acyclic nodes level by level.
    while (queue.length > 0) {
      const node = queue.shift()!;
      const neighbors = adjacency.get(node);
      if (!neighbors) continue;

      for (const neighbor of neighbors) {
        const nextDeg = (inDegree.get(neighbor) || 0) - 1;
        inDegree.set(neighbor, nextDeg);
        if (nextDeg === 0) queue.push(neighbor);
      }
    }

    // Any node with remaining positive in-degree is part of a cycle.
    const cycleFiles: string[] = [];
    for (const [node, deg] of inDegree) {
      if (deg > 0) cycleFiles.push(node);
    }

    return cycleFiles;
  };

  const allPathSet = new Set(allPaths);
  const levels = topologicalLevelSort(ctx.importMap);

  // Cycle diagnostic: compute actual cycle files rather than inferring from last topo level.
  if (isDev) {
    const cycleFiles = getImportCycleFiles(ctx.importMap);
    if (cycleFiles.length > 0) {
      console.log(`🔄 ${cycleFiles.length} files in import cycles (skipped for cross-file propagation)`);
```

`lookupRawReturnType` falls back to `options.importedReturnTypes`, but `importedReturnTypes` is currently built from `extractReturnTypeName(...)` (i.e., a processed/simple type name), not the raw declared return type. This breaks the `ReturnTypeLookup` contract for callers that need raw container types (e.g., for-loop element inference via `extractElementTypeFromString`). Consider either (a) storing raw return-type strings in `importedReturnTypes` and applying `extractReturnTypeName` only in `lookupReturnType`, or (b) not using `importedReturnTypes` as a fallback for `lookupRawReturnType`.
```suggestion
      // No unambiguous symbol-table match → no cross-file fallback for raw types
      return undefined;
```

