The retry path resets the module-level `conn`/`db`/`currentDbPath` *outside* `runWithSessionLock`. Another request can acquire the session lock between the failed attempt and this cleanup, then have its active connection closed/null’d out mid-operation. Move the close+state-reset into a short `runWithSessionLock` block (sleep can remain outside) so the globals are never mutated concurrently with another DB operation.
```suggestion
      await runWithSessionLock(async () => {
        // Close any stale connection before retrying so doInitLbug opens fresh
        try { if (conn) await conn.close(); } catch {}
        try { if (db) await db.close(); } catch {}
        conn = null;
        db = null;
        currentDbPath = null;
        ftsLoaded = false;
      });
```

`isAllowedOrigin` parses the origin with `new URL(origin)` but never checks the URL protocol. As written, a non-HTTP(S) origin like `ftp://10.0.0.1` would be treated as allowed because it falls into a private IP range. Consider explicitly rejecting any origin whose parsed protocol is not `http:` or `https:` before applying the private-network checks (and optionally add a unit test for this case).
```suggestion
    const url = new URL(origin);
    if (url.protocol !== 'http:' && url.protocol !== 'https:') {
      // Only HTTP(S) origins are allowed
      return false;
    }
    hostname = url.hostname;
```

`Renamed`/`Copied` change types are produced (via `STATUS_MAP`), but the weighted risk score only counts `Added`/`Modified`/`Deleted`. As a result, a diff consisting solely of renames/copies will report `risk_level: none`/`risk_score: 0`, which is misleading because renames can break imports/callers. Consider folding `Renamed`/`Copied` into the `Modified` bucket (or explicitly weighting them) when computing `weightedScore`.
```suggestion
    const renamedCount = changedSymbols.filter(s => s.change_type === 'Renamed').length;
    const copiedCount = changedSymbols.filter(s => s.change_type === 'Copied').length;
    const effectiveModifiedCount = modifiedCount + renamedCount + copiedCount;
    const weightedScore = (effectiveModifiedCount * 3) + (deletedCount * 5) + (addedCount * 0.1);
```

The dominant-type labeling logic reads `graph.getNodeAttribute(nodeId, 'labels')`, but nodes added in `buildGraphologyGraph` use the attribute key `type` (and `name`/`filePath`). As written, `labels` will be undefined and `dominantType` will never be selected, so this fallback won’t work. Use the existing `type` attribute (or ensure `labels` is actually set on nodes) when counting types.
```suggestion
    // Prefer explicit `type` attribute; fall back to `labels` if present
    const typeAttr = graph.getNodeAttribute(nodeId, 'type');
    const labelsAttr = typeAttr ? undefined : graph.getNodeAttribute(nodeId, 'labels');
    const nodeType = typeof typeAttr === 'string'
      ? typeAttr
      : (Array.isArray(labelsAttr)
          ? labelsAttr[0]
          : (typeof labelsAttr === 'string' ? labelsAttr : ''));
```

`synthesizeWildcardImportBindings()` scans *all* graph nodes to rebuild `exportedSymbolsByFile` and iterates the entire `ctx.importMap` every time it runs. Calling it once per chunk in the worker path risks O(chunks × graph_size) work on large repos. Consider caching exported symbols (or maintaining them incrementally) and/or synthesizing only for the importing files affected by the current chunk’s newly-resolved imports, then keep the final pass as a safety net.
```suggestion
          // NOTE: Per-chunk wildcard-import synthesis has been removed from the worker path
          // to avoid O(chunks × graph_size) behavior. A final/global synthesis pass should
          // run after all imports are resolved but before any global call resolution.
```

