This test file re-implements the security guard logic inline instead of importing the production helpers. That creates a real risk of tests passing while production behavior diverges (e.g., if the regex/allowlist changes). Consider exporting these guards from the production modules (or exposing them via a dedicated `securityGuards` module) so the tests exercise the actual implementation.
```suggestion
import { validLabel, validRelType } from '../../src/core/llm/tools';
import { isSafeId } from '../../src/components/ProcessesPanel';
import { isWriteQuery } from '../../src/core/lbug/lbug-adapter';

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Use the security guards from production so tests track the real behavior.
//
// Source locations:
//   validLabel / validRelType  -- gitnexus-web/src/core/llm/tools.ts
//   isSafeId                   -- gitnexus-web/src/components/ProcessesPanel.tsx
//   write-query guard (regex)  -- gitnexus-web/src/core/lbug/lbug-adapter.ts
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
```

`isSafeId` currently rejects `/`, but graph node IDs are generated as `${label}:${filePath}...` (see `generateId()`), so step IDs like `Function:src/foo.ts:bar` will almost always contain `/`. Filtering step IDs/process IDs with this regex will drop legitimate IDs and likely break process-edge queries/highlighting. Consider either (a) allowing `/` (and other path-safe chars like `@`) in the regex, or (b) avoiding string interpolation altogether by switching these queries to parameterized Cypher / prepared statements.
```suggestion
/** Validate that an ID contains only expected node identifier characters (no Cypher metacharacters or spaces, but allow path-safe chars like `/` and `@`) */
const isSafeId = (id: string): boolean => /^[a-zA-Z0-9_:.@/\-]+$/.test(id);
```

`loadGraphToLbug` recreates the in-memory DB/connection on every load by overwriting `db`/`conn`, but it doesn't close any previously-open connection/database first. This can leak WASM/DB resources across repo loads. Close the existing `conn`/`db` (or call `closeLbug()`) before reassigning, and consider whether `initPromise` should be updated to reflect the new connection.
```suggestion
  const BUFFER_POOL_SIZE = 512 * 1024 * 1024; // 512MB (mirror init)
  // Close any existing connection/database before creating new ones to avoid leaking WASM/DB resources
  if (conn) {
    try {
      if (typeof conn.close === 'function') {
        conn.close();
      }
    } catch (e) {
      if (import.meta.env.DEV) {
        console.warn('LadybugDB: Failed to close existing connection before reload:', e);
      }
    } finally {
      conn = null;
    }
  }
  if (db) {
    try {
      if (typeof db.close === 'function') {
        db.close();
      }
    } catch (e) {
      if (import.meta.env.DEV) {
        console.warn('LadybugDB: Failed to close existing database before reload:', e);
      }
    } finally {
      db = null;
    }
  }
```

The new `isSafeId` allowlist excludes `/`, but many node IDs in this app include file paths (e.g. `Function:src/app.ts:main:1`). Filtering step IDs with this regex will drop most valid IDs, causing the CALLS-edge queries to be skipped and process diagrams to lose real edges. Consider either widening the allowlist to include `/` for ID *values* (while still escaping `'`), or switching these `IN [...]` clauses to parameterized queries so you can keep a strict allowlist without breaking valid IDs.
```suggestion
/** Validate that an ID contains only expected node identifier characters (no Cypher metacharacters or spaces; slashes in file paths are allowed) */
const isSafeId = (id: string): boolean => /^[a-zA-Z0-9_:./\-]+$/.test(id);
```

The new disambiguation logic can incorrectly collapse truly-ambiguous results when there are multiple Class/Interface nodes with the same name (e.g., same class name in different packages). Because the label-typed query uses `LIMIT 1` and ignores `file_path`, it may pick an arbitrary Class and bypass the existing `status: 'ambiguous'` response. Consider only auto-selecting when exactly one preferred-label candidate exists among `symbols` (or when the ambiguity is specifically between Class/Interface vs Constructor), and include the `file_path` constraint when present so the preferred match is chosen from the same candidate set.
```suggestion
      const getKind = (s: any): string | undefined => (s.type || s[2]);
      let preferred: any = null;

      // First, try to disambiguate using the kinds already present in `symbols`.
      const preferredByKind = symbols.filter((s: any) => {
        const kind = getKind(s);
        return kind !== undefined && PREFER_LABELS.includes(kind);
      });

      // Only auto-select when exactly one preferred-label candidate exists.
      if (preferredByKind.length === 1) {
        preferred = preferredByKind[0];
      } else if (preferredByKind.length === 0) {
        // If we couldn't determine kinds from `symbols`, fall back to a label-typed
        // query, but restrict it to the current candidate set to avoid collapsing
        // truly ambiguous results from elsewhere in the graph.
        const candidateIds = symbols
          .map((s: any) => s.id || s[0])
          .filter((id: any) => id !== undefined && id !== null);

        for (const label of PREFER_LABELS) {
          const match = await executeParameterized(
            repo.id,
            `
          MATCH (n:\`${label}\`)
          WHERE n.name = $symName AND n.id IN $candidateIds
          RETURN n.id AS id
          LIMIT 2
        `,
            { symName: name!, candidateIds }
          ).catch(() => []);

          // Only auto-select if there is exactly one preferred candidate among
          // the current symbols; otherwise preserve ambiguity.
          if (match.length === 1) {
            preferred = symbols.find(
              (s: any) => (s.id || s[0]) === (match[0].id || match[0][0])
            );
            if (preferred) break;
          }
        }
      }
```

Incoming ref merge deduplicates only by `uid`, which can drop distinct relationships from the same caller (e.g., if a node both IMPORTS and CALLS the target, only the first row survives and the other category will be missing). Deduplicate by a compound key such as `(relType, uid)` (or dedupe per-category after `categorize`) to avoid losing data.
```suggestion
        // Deduplicate by (relType, uid) before merging to preserve distinct relationships
        const seenRelTypeUid = new Set(
