**Bug: MRO chain incorrectly falls through to parent on arity mismatch**

The MRO chain for Case 2 is ordered most-derived-first: `[Child, Parent, Grandparent, ...]`. When `Child::method()` is found but arity-incompatible with the call site, this code clears `memberDef` and `continue`s — walking to `Parent::method()` and potentially emitting a `CALLS` edge there if that ancestor happens to be arity-compatible.

This is incorrect. PHP always dispatches to the most-derived override. If the call is arity-incompatible with `Child::method`, PHP throws `ArgumentCountError` at runtime — it never silently redirects to an ancestor method. The resolver should emit **no edge** for this call.

The comment _"a subclass may shadow with a different arity"_ is also inverted: the chain walks *up* toward ancestors (less-derived), not down toward subclasses.

The fix is to `break` instead of `continue`, so that discovering an arity-incompatible most-derived definition terminates the chain walk and emits no edge:

