if (maxInjectedSiblings > 0 && injectedIds.size >= maxInjectedSiblings) {
  truncated = true;
  break;
}
...
if (truncated) {
  incompleteFiles.add(filePath);
  truncatedFiles++;
}
```

Per file, not per bucket — the truncation is per module scope, so marking the whole package would over-degrade the files that were fully injected. After the bucket loop, one aggregated `logger.warn` names the cap and how many files it hit, so the state is no longer silent (the per-file version would flood on a 400-file package).

Regression test added: `marks a file whose sibling set was truncated as visibility-incomplete`, plus the default-cap case which asserts the same for an unset env var. Both fail against the pre-fix implementation.

**[P1] Blanking a directive line that carries a block-comment terminator turns valid Swift into a parse error.**

This blank/keep decision consults only `insideMultilineString`; it never consults `state.blockCommentDepth`, which this file already tracks. So a line like `  #endif */` — the result of commenting out a conditional block, which is ordinary developer behavior — matches the regex at line 1 and gets erased *including the `*/`*.

Reproduced against the real vendored grammar:

```swift
class Foo {
  /* temporarily disabled:
  #if DEBUG
  func f() {}
  #endif */
  func g() {}
}
```

`hasError raw=false → preprocessed=true`. The comment is left unterminated and swallows `func g()` and the rest of the file. This PR converts previously-parseable Swift into a parse error.

It also contradicts this file's own doc comment at lines 124-127: *"Directive-looking lines inside block comments are still blanked: the Swift parser ignores comment interiors, so this remains parse-harmless."* It is not parse-harmless. A second, softer consequence of the same gap: `/** … */` doc comments lose blanked lines from the extracted `description`, because Swift reads block-doc text from the preprocessed tree (`BASE: "… Example: #if os(iOS) widget.useUIKit() #endif"` → `PR: "… Example: widget.useUIKit()"`).

**Fix:** add the depth check — `!insideMultilineString && state.blockCommentDepth === 0 && SWIFT_INDENTED_CONDITIONAL_DIRECTIVE_RE.test(...)`. Nothing needs blanking inside a comment; the grammar already ignores it. Add a regression with the closing delimiter on the directive-looking line.

