**[P1] Quadratic scan on a run of bare `#`: one 512 KB line stalls the parse worker for minutes.**

This loop walks the entire remaining `#` run at every index; `scanSwiftLine` at lines 93-99 walks the same run **again** inline; and when it isn't a raw-string opener, `index` advances by exactly one at line 105. For a run of length n that is Σ(n, n-1, …, 1) character comparisons.

Measured against the PR-head module:

```
n=4000   ms=39.9
n=8000   ms=153.0    (3.84×)
n=16000  ms=586.3    (3.83×)
n=32000  ms=2715.1   (4.63×)
n=64000  ms=10648.1  (3.92×)   <- 10.6 s for one 64 KB line
```

~4× per doubling is the textbook quadratic signature. The same total `#` count spread over many short lines takes 9.8 ms, and ordinary Swift-ish text is linear — so this is an isolated algorithmic bug, not a general property of the scanner, and it does not contradict the (correct) finding that general input is linear. It needs a long run of consecutive `#` on **one line in code position**: generated, minified, corrupt, or deliberately hostile `.swift` content, which GitNexus ingests by design.

The walker's default skip threshold is 512 KB (`src/core/ingestion/utils/max-file-size.ts:5`), so a **within-limits** file lands around ten minutes of pure preprocessing — a synchronous JS loop before any N-API call, so the worker can't post progress. That runs straight into the pool's idle-timeout → split → retry → quarantine ladder, ending in the file being silently excluded. Output length stays correct throughout; this is a stall, not a corruption.

**Fix:**

```ts
if (line[index] === '#') {
  let rawPoundCount = 0;
  while (line[index + rawPoundCount] === '#') rawPoundCount++;
  if (line[index + rawPoundCount] === '"') {
    index = skipRegularString(line, index, rawPoundCount);
  } else {
    index += rawPoundCount; // was: fell through to index++
  }
  continue;
}
```

and share the counted value with `rawPoundCountBeforeTripleQuote` so the run is measured once per position instead of twice. Per repo convention, any regression test should assert on a bounded operation count or a `Promise.race` timeout, not a wall-clock threshold.

`[reproduced]` — cross-engine (Codex swarm SB-1 and adversarial, Claude performance F1) and independently reproduced by the coordinator.

**Blocking.** This `return value` makes the docstring above untrue — it says the function coerces the variable "into a value Express accepts", and unrecognised strings are passed straight through. Express compiles `trust proxy` eagerly at `app.set` time (`api.ts:727`), so anything `proxy-addr` doesn't recognise throws there.

Reproduced against the Express 5.2.1 pinned in this tree:

| value | result |
|---|---|
| `garbage` | `invalid IP address: garbage` |
| `10.0.0.0/99` | `invalid range on address: 10.0.0.0/99` |
| `-1`, `1.5` | fall through as strings, throw the same way |
| `9`&times;400 | `Infinity` — accepted silently |

To be fair, this is not an uncaught crash: `serve.ts:35-62` wraps `createServer` and exits 1 via `serve.startFailed`. But the operator only sees `invalid IP address: yes`, which never names `GITNEXUS_TRUST_PROXY`, and on Render that's a redeploy loop with no pointer to the cause.

The numeric branch is the mirror image: unbounded. I reproduced `resolveTrustProxy('9'.repeat(400))` returning `Infinity`, which Express accepts and which then reports `trust proxy` true at hop 0, 1, 5, 100 and 10000 — it trusts every hop, i.e. exactly the `true` behaviour the docstring three lines above warns against, reached by one extra keystroke. `true` itself is accepted with no warning too, and because `createRouteLimiter` (`validation.ts:151-165`) supplies its own `keyGenerator`, `express-rate-limit`'s built-in alarm for this setting never fires — I saw 6/6 requests return 200 against a limit of 3 by rotating `X-Forwarded-For`, silently.

This also puts the variable out of step with every neighbour in the same README table (`GITNEXUS_LBUG_BUFFER_POOL_SIZE`, `GITNEXUS_LBUG_MAX_DB_SIZE`, `GITNEXUS_MAX_CALLABLE_VALUE_TARGETS` all warn and fall back), and with the sibling function added in this same commit — `createPublicOriginMatcher` returns `undefined` on bad input precisely because "an invalid origin must never widen the allow-list" (`middleware.ts:57-58`).

`proxy-addr` is already an Express dependency and exports `compile`, so:

```ts
try {
  proxyaddr.compile(value.split(',').map((s) => s.trim()));
  return value;
} catch {
  logger.warn({ [TRUST_PROXY_ENV]: value }, 'invalid value; falling back to the default');
  return DEFAULT_TRUST_PROXY;
}
```

Bounding the numeric branch to something sane (1&ndash;16) and falling into the same path otherwise closes the `Infinity` hole. A `logger.warn` when the resolved value is `true` is cheap insurance, accepting `yes`/`no`/`on`/`off` removes a whole class of typo, and the docstring needs correcting either way.

If someone sets `GITNEXUS_PUBLIC_ORIGIN=app.example.com`, `expectedProtocol` stays undefined and `http://app.example.com` gets write access plus CORS reads. On an https deployment that's a downgrade path.

The bare-host form is for Render/Fly service discovery iirc, and those are always https, so I think we can just default it?

```suggestion
  // A bare host stays permissive on the port, since platform service-discovery
  // fields resolve to a hostname with neither, but defaults to https.
  const expectedProtocol = `${scheme ?? 'https'}:`;
```
